<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-spot-the-difference">
  <title>Varieties of plots, developing plots</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>"I repeat, as long as you have studied the theory hard enough."</p>
      <p>"And what good's theory going to be in the real world?" said Harry loudly, his fist in the air again.</p>
      <p>--- <em>Professor Dolores Umbridge and Harry Potter in "Harry Potter and the Order of the Phoenix"</em></p>
    </blockquote>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-varying-plots">
    <title>Varying various plots</title>
    <p>
      There is a wide variety of plot types available (e.g., @holtz:2018 or @schwab:2021). Each plot type can also
      be drawn in many different ways: varying size, aspect ratio, scales, colour, symbols, styles, whatever. A key
      message is that drawing a variety of plots, and varying the plots drawn, can provide more insights than
      searching for a single "optimal" display.
    </p>
    <p>
      Approaches vary: some prefer to study one graphic deeply before moving on; others scan many graphics then return
      to selected ones. Being swamped by too many graphics can be as unsatisfactory as not having enough.
    </p>

    <p>
      Not all graphics are the same. There are different plot types, different kinds of displayed data, and different
      uses for graphics: exploration, explanation, and exposition. Accordingly, the same display may be interpreted in
      different ways depending on context and on what kind of data it represents.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-s100mscat">
      <image source="images/s100mScat-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Four different kinds of scatterplot for Olympic 100 m freestyle gold/silver data.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      These four views show different aspects of the same data: time series by year, men-versus-women winner
      comparisons, mean-difference structure for medal gaps, and residual-versus-fitted structure for a simple model.
      The displays reveal features that would be easy to miss if only one plot were inspected.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-finch2box">
      <image source="images/finch2box-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Boxplots of beak measurements (above) and beak height by species (below) on Isabela Island.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      A set of boxplots in parallel could represent multiple variables or one variable conditioned on a grouping
      variable. It is important to indicate what kind of plot is being shown. The danger lies in glancing quickly at
      isolated graphics and not checking what they display.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ensembles-of-plots">
    <title>Ensembles of plots</title>
    <p>
      Thanks to trellis plots (@becker:1996), lattice plots (@sarkar:2008), and faceting (@wilkinson:1999 and
      @wickham:2016), displays of small multiples are well understood. By comparison, ensembles made up of different
      types of plots have been less discussed. @unwin:2018 suggest that the key element is coherence: having a
      coherent theme, coherent alignment, coherent look, and consistent scales and formatting.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-commfmedx">
      <image source="images/comMFmedX-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Median times for men and women for up/down Comrades races and numbers finishing.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      In this ensemble, the graphics are aligned horizontally and vertically, as are the scales. Colours above and
      below refer to different grouping structures.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-qlensx">
      <image source="images/qlEnsX-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Complex ensemble comparing dropout rates, initial scores, and quality-of-life question means.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      This ensemble compares three distinct information sets with different plot forms: dropout proportions,
      distributional comparisons for initial measurements, and ten-question means over time by group.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-early-vs-later">
    <title>Differences between early drafts and later graphics</title>
    <p>
      Quickly drawn default graphics can reveal valuable information. Taking time to improve them can make information
      easier to see and uncover additional structure. It is instructive to compare pairs of initial and better graphics
      to study what changed and why.
    </p>

    <p>
      "Initial" often means after some data cleaning; first graphics frequently reveal further data problems that
      require additional wrangling. There can be many early and many later graphics. There is no final graphic; there
      are always potential alternatives.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-early-later-1912">
      <title>1912 Democratic Convention</title>
      <figure xml:id="fig-candvsx">
        <image source="images/candvsX-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Initial convention-voting plot.</p></caption>
      </figure>
      <figure xml:id="fig-candvsx2">
        <image source="images/candvsX2-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Amended convention-voting plot with focused design choices.</p></caption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The first plot shows many candidates but only a few serious ones, Clark's early lead, Wilson's steady gain,
        and a decisive breakthrough. The second plot emphasises key changes and timing around adjournments.
      </p>
      <p>Changes include:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><p>The simple order of ballots on the horizontal scale was replaced by estimated ballot times.</p></li>
        <li><p>Only candidates receiving over 35 votes in at least one ballot were included, reducing clutter from minor candidates.</p></li>
        <li><p>The plot background was coloured white when the convention was in session and grey when it was not.</p></li>
        <li><p>The aspect ratio was changed to 0.5; time series are generally better wider than tall.</p></li>
        <li><p>The dataset was expanded to include ballots where candidates received zero votes so lines were drawn correctly.</p></li>
        <li><p>Vote changes were shown as vertical lines at ballot times rather than sloping lines between ballots.</p></li>
        <li><p>Points were drawn for each ballot to emphasise when and how often ballots took place.</p></li>
        <li><p>Horizontal lines were added at two-thirds support and at 50 percent support.</p></li>
        <li><p>A colour-blind scale was used instead of the default colour scale.</p></li>
        <li><p>Candidate plotting order and legend order were changed to make overlap and line identification easier.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-early-later-gapminder">
      <title>Gapminder life expectancies</title>
      <figure xml:id="fig-gaplex-1">
        <image source="images/gapLEX-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>All-country life expectancy display.</p></caption>
      </figure>
      <figure xml:id="fig-gaplex-2">
        <image source="images/gapLEX-2.png"/>
        <caption><p>Faceted regional life expectancy display.</p></caption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The initial display shows long-run increase and occasional sharp changes. The faceted version reveals stronger
        regional structure and local outliers.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><p>Countries coloured by region.</p></li>
        <li><p>Regions plotted separately to reveal local differences.</p></li>
        <li><p>Some small incomplete-country series excluded.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-early-later-electric">
      <title>Electric car charging</title>
      <figure xml:id="fig-elecstat12">
        <image source="images/elecStat12-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Default and amended displays of charging-station usage over time.</p></caption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The first plot shows late starts and low-use stations. The second plot highlights phase boundaries and
        location-level usage differences worth investigating.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><p>A high aspect ratio was used for the amended plot to show station-level points more clearly.</p></li>
        <li><p>Charging stations were sorted by location to compare neighbouring-station use.</p></li>
        <li><p>Locations were ordered by first use to match study development.</p></li>
        <li><p>Charging stations were ordered within location to show installation development.</p></li>
        <li><p>Facility types were coloured to highlight group differences.</p></li>
        <li><p>A dashed red line marked the switch from test phase to analysis phase.</p></li>
        <li><p>The vertical axis label and individual station IDs were dropped; location ID labels were added.</p></li>
        <li><p>Date formatting and labelling were improved for context; the uninformative horizontal-axis label was removed.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-early-later-gay-rights">
      <title>Gay rights</title>
      <figure xml:id="fig-gaycix">
        <image source="images/gayCIX-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>State support with confidence intervals: default and ordered versions.</p></caption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The amended plot clarifies regional and within-region structure, highlights the 50 percent threshold, and
        improves comparability.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><p>States were sorted by Census Bureau region so local comparisons are easier.</p></li>
        <li><p>States were ordered by estimate within region to facilitate comparison.</p></li>
        <li><p>Estimates were coloured by region to differentiate groups.</p></li>
        <li><p>Points were drawn larger and intervals drawn as straight lines without end bars to reduce clutter.</p></li>
        <li><p>Horizontal scaling was changed to percentages and limits set to [0,100%], with breakpoints including 50%.</p></li>
        <li><p>Axis-name labels were dropped to leave more space for the graphic.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-early-later-spaceflight">
      <title>Human space flight</title>
      <figure xml:id="fig-flighttz">
        <image source="images/flighttz-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Mission time versus year: raw and log-transformed comparison.</p></caption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The log-scale display reveals structure in short and long missions and clarifies programme-related groupings.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><p>Log transformation used to reveal more structure.</p></li>
        <li><p>Breaks chosen for interpretable time periods.</p></li>
        <li><p>Space-station programme colouring used for subgroup exploration.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-early-later-euro2020">
      <title>Euro 2020</title>
      <figure xml:id="fig-uefa20x">
        <image source="images/uefa20X-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Default faceted bar display of players' league countries.</p></caption>
      </figure>
      <figure xml:id="fig-uefa20y">
        <image source="images/uefa20Y-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Amended display with ordering, grouping, and simplified guides.</p></caption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The amended display supports easier team and league pattern identification by ordering and grouping choices.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><p>Teams were ordered by the percentage of players in their own national league.</p></li>
        <li><p>Bars were ordered by total players from each league; leagues with fewer than nine players were grouped as Other.</p></li>
        <li><p>Colours close to country colours were used instead of defaults.</p></li>
        <li><p>The legend was dropped, as labels already indicate colour meaning and the legend used too much space.</p></li>
        <li><p>The number of facet rows was set to four for a better balanced display and more vertical label space.</p></li>
        <li><p>Axis-title labels were dropped; the horizontal-axis scale was dropped with extra end space added; gridlines removed.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-characteristic-changes">
    <title>Characteristic changes</title>
    <ol>
      <li><p>Grouping information supports understanding by chunking complex structure.</p></li>
      <li><p>Aspect ratio choices are often critical.</p></li>
      <li><p>Ordering is an effective way to emphasize patterns.</p></li>
      <li><p>Colour is powerful and should be chosen deliberately.</p></li>
      <li><p>Reducing clutter and making space is usually worthwhile.</p></li>
      <li><p>Scaling and labelling should be sufficient but not excessive.</p></li>
      <li><p>Time scaling and time labels require care.</p></li>
      <li><p>Guidelines for critical values can improve interpretation.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-classifying-graphics">
    <title>Classifying graphics</title>
    <p>
      Graphics may be classified in different ways and a number of terms are used. Data graphics generally refers to
      displays of raw data. Model graphics implies displays associated with models. Multivariate graphics includes
      displays showing many variables. Interactive graphics describes displays that can be directly manipulated.
      Dynamic graphics means displays that can be set in motion. There is overlap between interactive and dynamic
      graphics, but the emphasis differs.
    </p>

    <p>
      Technical properties and data background are not the only determinants of use. Setting matters: specialist
      publication versus mass-media item, attentive audience versus competing attention, and high versus low assumed
      domain knowledge.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-spot-main-points">
      <title>Main points</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Graphics that look similar can represent different tasks and data contexts.</p></li>
        <li><p>Ensembles work best when theme, alignment, scales, and formatting are coherent.</p></li>
        <li><p>Describing why a plot was changed is often more informative than describing how.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
